# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Supabase — TWO PROJECTS, ONE PER ENVIRONMENT
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Development and the deployment have separate Supabase projects. A script run
# on a laptop therefore cannot reach the rows holding real grades, and a new
# migration meets real data somewhere other than the deployment first.
#
# The five values below are a *pair* of sets. This file's values are the
# development project's. The deployment's live in its Vercel environment, and a
# copy of the four that a terminal command needs lives in
# `.env.deployment.local`:
#
#   DATABASE_URL, DIRECT_URL, NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL, SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY
#
# Four rather than five because that file configures commands rather than a
# running application, and nothing run from a terminal reads the publishable
# key. `npm run db:status:deployment`, `db:deploy:deployment`, and
# `setup:storage:deployment` read it and run the ordinary script against the
# deployment; `scripts/with-deployment-env.ts` explains what it refuses and why.
# Nothing else reaches the deployment's database, which is the point — the
# environment is named by the command rather than by an edit to this file.
#
# NOT `.env.production.local`. Next.js loads that name automatically whenever
# NODE_ENV is production, ahead of `.env.local`, which would leave a local
# `npm run build` or `npm start` as the one command still reaching the
# deployment's database.
#
# Both projects need the same dashboard setup, and each needs its OWN GitHub
# OAuth application: an OAuth application has one authorization callback URL,
# and the callback belongs to the Supabase project rather than to the machine —
# `https://<project-ref>.supabase.co/auth/v1/callback`. Localhost belongs in the
# development project's Site URL and Redirect URLs instead. Disable the Email
# provider on both. This is the same shape of problem as the two GitHub Apps
# described further down, for the same reason: one URL per registration.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Update these with your Supabase details from your project settings > API
# https://app.supabase.com/project/_/settings/api
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=your-project-url
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=your-publishable-or-anon-key

# Service role key — Project Settings > API. Server-side ONLY; it bypasses RLS.
# Never expose it to the browser and never prefix it with NEXT_PUBLIC_.
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=your-service-role-key

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Prisma / Postgres — Project Settings > Database > Connection string
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Take both strings from THIS project's own Connect dialog rather than editing
# another project's. The pooler hostname carries a region and a numeric prefix
# assigned per project, so swapping a project reference into the wrong host
# fails with `FATAL: (ENOTFOUND) tenant/user postgres.<ref> not found`. The
# username differs between the two forms as well: the pooler wants
# `postgres.<project-ref>`, the direct `db.<ref>.supabase.co` endpoint just
# `postgres` — and that direct endpoint answers over IPv6 only unless the IPv4
# add-on is enabled, which is the other half of why both values here are pooler
# strings.
#
# Runtime queries. Supavisor TRANSACTION pooler (port 6543) — the right choice
# for serverless functions on Vercel, which open many short-lived connections.
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://postgres.your-project-ref:YOUR-DB-PASSWORD@aws-0-your-region.pooler.supabase.com:6543/postgres

# Migrations only. Supavisor SESSION pooler (port 5432). The transaction pooler
# cannot run the DDL and advisory locks that `prisma migrate` needs.
DIRECT_URL=postgresql://postgres.your-project-ref:YOUR-DB-PASSWORD@aws-0-your-region.pooler.supabase.com:5432/postgres

# Only needed when authoring NEW migrations. Supabase can't host a shadow DB
# over the pooler, so point this at a local Postgres. Either works:
#   Postgres.app (already installed): createdb mls_lms_shadow
#   Docker:  docker run -d -e POSTGRES_PASSWORD=shadow -p 55432:5432 postgres:15-alpine
# SHADOW_DATABASE_URL=postgresql://benspector@127.0.0.1:5432/mls_lms_shadow
# SHADOW_DATABASE_URL=postgresql://postgres:shadow@127.0.0.1:55432/postgres
#
# Note: `prisma migrate dev` does NOT work on this project — it offers to reset
# the real database. See the authoring recipe at the bottom of prisma.config.ts.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# GitHub App — see the GitHub App setup section of the README
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# TWO APPS, ONE PER ENVIRONMENT. A GitHub App has exactly one webhook URL, and
# GitHub cannot reach localhost, so a single app cannot serve both development
# and the deployment without its URL being edited on every switch. The four
# values below are therefore a *pair* of sets:
#
#   marcy-lms       webhook → https://<your-deployment>/api/webhooks/github
#                   values live in the deployment's environment
#   marcy-lms-dev   webhook → your smee.io channel (GITHUB_WEBHOOK_PROXY_URL)
#                   values live in this file
#
# Mirror the production app's configuration when creating the development one:
# subscribe to the `pull_request` event only, and grant Administration: write,
# Contents: write, Members: write, Metadata: read, Pull requests: write.
# Install it on the student-repository organization AND on every organization
# holding grading assets — the rubric repository, and any repository an assignment
# names as its answer keys. Every environment reads those over the API, so an app
# that cannot see them cannot grade. Which installation reads a given repository is
# resolved from the repository's owner, so installing it is all that is needed.
#
# From the app's settings page. The private key is a downloaded .pem file; paste
# its contents with literal \n between lines, or paste it as a multi-line quoted
# value. The code converts literal \n sequences to real newlines.
GITHUB_APP_ID=
GITHUB_APP_PRIVATE_KEY=
# A secret you choose, entered identically in the app's webhook settings. Used to
# verify that webhook requests actually came from GitHub.
#
# Use a DIFFERENT secret for each app. Sharing one saves nothing — it is typed
# once per app either way — and it is the only thing distinguishing a genuine
# delivery from a forged one, so a leak from a development machine would
# otherwise compromise the deployment's verification too.
GITHUB_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
# The installation id for the organization, visible in the URL when viewing the
# installation: .../settings/installations/<id>
# `npx tsx --conditions=react-server scripts/list-installations.ts` prints them.
GITHUB_APP_INSTALLATION_ID=

# Development only, and only for the app whose webhook points at smee.io. Create
# a channel at https://smee.io/new, set it as that app's webhook URL, and put it
# here; `npm run dev:webhook` then forwards deliveries to the dev server.
#
# Leave this UNSET in the deployment, which needs no forwarder.
#
# Worth knowing: smee.io answers GitHub with 200 whether or not anything is
# listening, so a delivery that arrives while the forwarder is not running is
# recorded as a success and then dropped. If a push seems to have been ignored,
# check that `npm run dev:webhook` was running, and redeliver from the app's
# Advanced settings page.
# GITHUB_WEBHOOK_PROXY_URL=https://smee.io/xxxxxxxxxxxx

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# E2B — the sandbox student code runs in
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# From https://e2b.dev/dashboard. Server-side only; it can create and destroy
# sandboxes. Never prefix it with NEXT_PUBLIC_.
#
# This key is NOT passed into the sandbox. Nothing from process.env is: the
# sandbox runs code written by students, and the GitHub installation token in
# this same environment carries write access to every repository in the
# organization.
E2B_API_KEY=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# AI autograding
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Which model writes the grading reports. Defaults to groq when unset.
#   claude  — claude-sonnet-5. Caches the system prompt, so repeated submissions of
#             the same assignment bill the rubric and sample report at a fraction
#             of the normal input price.
#   groq    — openai/gpt-oss-120b. Free tier, but its 8,000 tokens-per-minute
#             ceiling is below what a frontend assignment needs: those prompts
#             carry several answer keys and reach roughly 12,000 tokens.
GRADING_LLM_PROVIDER=claude
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
# Optional override; the code picks a current model when this is unset.
# ANTHROPIC_MODEL=
# low | medium | high | xhigh. Defaults to high. Thinking is billed as output and
# output is most of what a report costs, so this is the significant cost lever —
# and it trades against grading quality. See the cost table in plan.md.
# GRADING_LLM_EFFORT=high
GROQ_API_KEY=

# Where the rubric, agent rules, sample reports, and answer keys come from.
#
# REQUIRED in every environment, including development. owner/repo of the private
# repository holding rubric.md, agent-rules.md, and the sample reports — the
# program-wide prompt code, read through the GitHub App. Files are fetched at the
# resolved commit SHA and cached under it, so a run cannot read half its rubric
# from before a push and half from after.
#
# It does NOT name where the answer keys are. An assignment names the repository
# its own reference solutions live in, in a column, so a cohort can keep them
# wherever it likes.
#
# There is no local-clone option. There was one, selected by GRADING_ASSETS_PATH,
# and it was removed: every later source of rubrics and keys is external — Google
# Drive for non-repository assignments — so reading from disk was never going to
# generalize, and two implementations of every read and list is a standing risk
# that development and deployment disagree about what exists. Setting
# GRADING_ASSETS_PATH now fails loudly rather than being ignored.
#
# The cost, which is real: tuning the rubric means committing and pushing, and
# waiting up to a minute for the new commit to be picked up. Push to a branch and
# point GRADING_ASSETS_REF at it to iterate without touching the default branch.
GRADING_ASSETS_REPO=The-Marcy-Lab-School/swe-assignment-grading-guides
# Optional, and rarely needed. The installation that reads a repository is resolved
# from its owner: a GitHub App is installed per organization with its own id and
# token, and an assignment may name an answer-key repository in an organization no
# variable here mentions, so the app asks itself which of its installations covers
# a given owner. Set this only to override that for the assets repository above.
# `npx tsx --conditions=react-server scripts/list-installations.ts` prints them.
GRADING_ASSETS_INSTALLATION_ID=
# Optional. A branch or tag to read instead of the default branch — pin grading to
# a known revision while the rubric is being revised on main.
# GRADING_ASSETS_REF=main

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Seed data (optional — all have defaults)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Both accounts must already exist as Supabase Auth users. The student account
# needs a linked GitHub identity, because accepting an assignment names the
# repository after their GitHub login.
# SEED_INSTRUCTOR_EMAIL=
# SEED_STUDENT_EMAIL=
# The organization the seeded assignment's repositories are created in. Read by
# the seed only — the running application takes each assignment's organization
# from its own row, so there is nothing to set in a deployment.
# SEED_GITHUB_ORG=marcy-lms
# The repository name only, without the organization — the seed prefixes it with
# SEED_GITHUB_ORG. Setting a full "owner/repo" value here produces
# "org/owner/repo", which is not rejected but is wrong.
# SEED_TEMPLATE_REPO=swe-1-4-loops
